Spain is one of the few countries that bans hitchhiking by name rather than merely banning pedestrians. [https://www.boe.es/buscar/act.php?id=BOE-A-2003-23514 Article 125 of the ''Reglamento General de Circulación''] says pedestrians may not walk on an ''autopista'' or ''autovía'' and may not "make requests for passage on any stretch of them, including the toll station forecourts" — so on those roads the thumb itself is the offence, service areas and peajes included. Article 94.1.g) separately allows vehicles to stop on an autopista or autovía only in the areas provided for it, and article 38 keeps bicycles, mopeds and animal-drawn vehicles off them entirely. Everywhere else — ''carreteras nacionales'', ''comarcales'' and ordinary streets — hitchhiking is not regulated at all and is perfectly legal. So work the ''carretera nacional'' network, or ask at the last petrol station ''before'' the motorway starts, rather than standing anywhere on the autovía.
